const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/washer-repair/samsung-not-spinning/";

const title = "Samsung Washer Not Spinning? Causes & Fix";
const description = "Samsung washer won't spin? Usually an unbalanced load (UR/UB), a drain problem (5C/SC), or the door lock (dC). Same-day in Los Angeles, $89 diagnostic.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why won't my Samsung washer spin?",
    a: "The most common reasons on a Samsung are an unbalanced load, a drain problem the washer has to clear before it can spin, a door that won't lock, or a worn drive belt or suspension. Samsung usually tells you which: UR or UB means it detected an unbalanced load and couldn't get up to spin speed; 5C, SC, or nd point at a drain fault (it won't spin on a full tub); dC means the door isn't locking. If there's no code, a worn belt, worn suspension rods, or the drive motor/rotor are the mechanical suspects. Redistributing the load and checking the drain are homeowner-safe; belt, suspension, and motor work is a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What does UR (or UB) mean on my Samsung washer?",
    a: "UR and UB are Samsung's unbalanced-load codes — the washer sensed the drum was off-balance and stopped ramping up to spin speed to avoid banging and 'walking' across the floor. It's the single most common reason a Samsung 'won't spin,' and it's often not a fault at all: a single heavy item (a bath mat, a hoodie), a small load that clumps to one side, or a machine that isn't level will trigger it. Open the door, redistribute the load evenly, make sure the washer is level and the shipping bolts were removed at install, and run a spin. If it balances and spins, nothing's broken. If UR keeps coming back on balanced loads, the suspension rods or springs may be worn."
  },
  {
    q: "Why won't my Samsung spin if it won't drain?",
    a: "A washer won't spin on top of a tub full of water — it can't balance and would sling water everywhere — so the control drains first, then spins. If your Samsung shows 5C, SC, or nd (drain codes) along with not spinning, the real fault is the drain, not the spin. Clear the drain — check the pump filter behind the bottom-front panel, the drain hose for a kink, and that the hose isn't shoved too far down the standpipe — and the spin usually returns. Our Samsung washer error codes page covers the 5C/SC/nd drain family in detail."
  },
  {
    q: "What does dC mean, and how does it stop the spin?",
    a: "dC is Samsung's door-lock code: the washer can't confirm the door is locked, so it won't start the spin (or sometimes the cycle at all) for safety. Check that the door is fully shut and nothing — a garment, the seal — is caught in it, then try again. If the door is clearly closed but dC persists, the door lock assembly has likely failed and needs replacing, which is a technician job. A Samsung won't spin with an unlocked or unconfirmed door by design, so dC is a genuine stop, not a glitch to ignore."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Samsung that won't spin myself?",
    a: "The common causes, often yes. Redistribute an unbalanced load, level the machine, confirm the shipping bolts are out, clear the drain (pump filter and hose), and make sure the door shuts fully — those cover unbalance, drain, and door issues, which are most no-spin cases. What to leave to a tech: a worn drive belt, worn suspension rods or springs (persistent UR on balanced loads), the door lock assembly, or the drive motor/rotor. Always unplug before checking the drain filter, and expect some standing water."
  }
];

