/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A no-spin Samsung is usually unbalance, drain, or the door lock — and the code names it.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Samsung washer won't spin, the causes are an unbalanced load, a drain problem it must clear before spinning, a door that won't lock, or a worn belt or suspension. Samsung usually tells you which: UR or UB is an unbalanced load; 5C, SC, or nd is a drain fault (it won't spin on a full tub); dC is the door not locking. With no code, a worn drive belt, worn suspension rods, or the motor/rotor are the mechanical suspects. The good news is that the two most common — unbalance and drain — are often homeowner-fixable: redistribute the load, level the machine, clear the drain. Belt, suspension, door lock, and motor work is a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Start with the code.</strong> UR/UB → balance the load. 5C/SC/nd → clear the drain. dC → check the door. No code → mechanical, likely a tech visit. It saves you chasing the wrong part.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 UNBALANCE -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · UR / UB — the unbalanced-load code</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The #1 reason a Samsung "won't spin" — and often not a fault.</h2>

    <p>UR (and UB on some models) means the washer sensed the drum was off-balance and deliberately stopped ramping up to full spin speed — a safety so it doesn't bang and 'walk' across the floor. It's the most common no-spin complaint on Samsung, and frequently nothing is broken:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>A single heavy item</strong> — a bath mat, a hoodie, one towel — throws the drum off-center. Add a couple more items to balance it.</li>
      <li><strong>A small load that clumps</strong> to one side. Redistribute it evenly around the drum.</li>
      <li><strong>The washer isn't level,</strong> or the shipping bolts were never removed at install. Level it; confirm the bolts are out.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Balance the load and run a spin — if it goes, you're done. If UR keeps returning on loads that <em>are</em> balanced and level, the suspension rods or springs are likely worn, and that's a tech repair. The full code list is on our <a href="/services/washer-repair/samsung-error-codes/">Samsung washer error codes</a> page.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Samsung won't spin</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Unbalanced load — UR / UB (check first)</h3>
      <p>The most common trigger. Redistribute the load, level the machine, confirm shipping bolts are out. Only a repair if UR persists on balanced loads (worn suspension).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Drain fault — 5C / SC / nd</h3>
      <p>It won't spin on a full tub, so a drain problem reads as a no-spin. Clear the pump filter and drain hose. See the drain codes on the Samsung error-codes page.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Door lock — dC</h3>
      <p>The washer won't spin unless the door locks. Check nothing's caught in it; if it's shut and dC persists, the door lock assembly has failed. Tech repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Worn suspension rods / springs</h3>
      <p>Worn suspension lets the drum move too much, so it can't reach spin speed even on a balanced load — persistent UR is the tell. Tech repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Drive belt or motor / rotor</h3>
      <p>A worn or broken drive belt, or a motor/rotor fault, stops the spin with no balance or drain code. A tech diagnosis with a meter.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before it's a repair.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Read the code</strong> — UR/UB, 5C/SC/nd, or dC tells you which path to take.</li>
      <li><strong>Redistribute the load</strong> evenly and add items to balance a single heavy piece; run a spin-only cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Level the washer</strong> and confirm the shipping bolts were removed when it was installed.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the drain</strong> if you see a drain code — unplug first, check the pump filter behind the bottom-front panel and the drain hose.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door</strong> shuts fully with nothing caught in the seal.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> balancing, leveling, drain filter, and the door are safe. Worn suspension, the door lock assembly, the drive belt, and the motor/rotor are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 SIBLING -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Same symptom on other brands</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">No-spin is a shared story — the code system differs.</h2>

    <p>Every brand drains before it spins and locks the door before it spins, so unbalance, drain, and door faults cause no-spin across the board — what differs is the code system. On a GE, for example, the lid switch and imbalance sensing work a little differently; see our <a href="/services/washer-repair/ge-not-spinning/">GE washer not spinning</a> page. If your Samsung's issue is really the drain, our general <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a> guide walks the full drain path.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Balanced, level, drain clear — still won't spin?</h2>

    <p>When the load is balanced, the machine is level, the drain is clear, and the door shuts but the Samsung still won't spin, the fault is mechanical — worn suspension, the door lock, the drive belt, or the motor. Our techs test each with a meter and carry the common Samsung suspension and belt parts for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the <a href="/services/washer-repair/wont-spin/">washer won't spin</a> guide (all brands), the <a href="/services/washer-repair/samsung-error-codes/">Samsung washer error codes</a> umbrella, and <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a> if it's really a drain fault. Sibling: <a href="/services/washer-repair/ge-not-spinning/">GE not spinning</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/washer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/washer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/washer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung washer not spinning: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Samsung washer won't spin? We'll find the real cause.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

